
Meeting created at: 15th Oct, 2026 - 10:33 PM1
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:30,110
Speaker 1: You may have heard people call it the white man's religion, brought into Africa as a tool for colonization, control and domination.

2
00:00:30,990 --> 00:00:40,750
Speaker 1: But what if I told you that Christianity's roots in Africa and its influence on the history of the world reach far beyond the stories you may have heard?

3
00:00:41,710 --> 00:00:53,800
Speaker 1: From the humble streets of Jerusalem to the grand temples of the Roman Empire, the early Church began with a single movement, the life, death and resurrection of Jesus Christ.

4
00:00:54,600 --> 00:01:02,040
Speaker 1: What followed was not just a religious movement, but a revolution that would change the world forever.

5
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:09,400
Speaker 1: But how did a small group of followers of a crucified man go on to shape an empire?

6
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:23,150
Speaker 1: Let's travel back to the first century and uncover the origins of the early Church, a time filled with passion and sacrifice and the birth of a faith that would soon transform civilizations.

7
00:01:24,990 --> 00:01:29,710
Speaker 1: At the heart of the early church story is the life and resurrection of Jesus Christ.

8
00:01:30,510 --> 00:01:42,270
Speaker 1: Around 30 A.D. this charismatic preacher, whom his followers believed to be the Son of God, began preaching messages of love, salvation and the kingdom of God.

9
00:01:42,590 --> 00:01:57,550
Speaker 1: But his radical ideas were met with opposition, leading to his crucifixion around 33 A.D. yet it was his resurrection three days later that set the foundation for what would soon become Christianity.

10
00:01:58,590 --> 00:02:04,350
Speaker 1: The apostles, the closest followers of Jesus, spread his teachings far and wide.

11
00:02:04,590 --> 00:02:08,990
Speaker 1: And this is where the story of the early Church truly begins.

12
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:16,200
Speaker 1: After the ascension of Jesus into heaven, his followers were left in Jerusalem.

13
00:02:16,520 --> 00:02:19,080
Speaker 1: But then something extraordinary happened.

14
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:29,320
Speaker 1: On the day of Pentecost, the Holy Spirit descended upon the apostles, empowering them to speak in tongues and spread the message of Jesus Christ.

15
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:36,680
Speaker 1: This event, around 3033 A.D. is seen as the birth of the Christian Church.

16
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:45,520
Speaker 1: From there, the apostles went out, facing tremendous challenges, but also gaining followers by the thousands.

17
00:02:46,080 --> 00:02:51,200
Speaker 1: But how did a small group in Jerusalem transform into a worldwide movement?

18
00:02:52,400 --> 00:03:00,400
Speaker 1: As Christianity spread, it faced fierce persecution from both the Jewish authorities and the Roman Empire.

19
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:09,440
Speaker 1: By 64 A.D. emperor Nero blamed Christians for the Great Fire of Rome, launching brutal persecution.

20
00:03:10,160 --> 00:03:14,320
Speaker 1: Yet despite the opposition, the Church continued to grow.

21
00:03:15,120 --> 00:03:23,760
Speaker 1: The Apostle Paul, one of the most influential early missionaries, traveled across the Roman Empire spreading the message of Jesus.

22
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:32,400
Speaker 1: His letters, many of which formed the New Testament, laid the foundation for Christian theology and practice.

23
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:34,960
Speaker 1: But what were these teachings?

24
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:40,000
Speaker 1: And how did they resonate with the diverse peoples of the Roman world?

25
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:45,360
Speaker 1: As the Church grew, it began to solidify its beliefs.

26
00:03:46,079 --> 00:03:55,280
Speaker 1: Early Christians had to define what they believed, especially in the face of heresies, false teachings that threatened the unity of the faith.

27
00:03:56,570 --> 00:04:08,170
Speaker 1: In the second century, writings by figures like Ignatius of Antioch and Irenaeus of Lyons defended Christian doctrine and affirmed the authority of the apostles.

28
00:04:09,290 --> 00:04:16,649
Speaker 1: The Church also began to form the New Testament canon, selecting books that were believed to be divinely inspired.

29
00:04:17,290 --> 00:04:26,760
Speaker 1: But it wasn't until the 4th century that the key doctrines of the Church such as the nature of Jesus Christ and the Trinity were officially recognized.

30
00:04:27,560 --> 00:04:35,480
Speaker 1: Fast forward to 312 AD when Constantine, the Roman Emperor had a vision that changed the course of history.

31
00:04:36,520 --> 00:04:45,000
Speaker 1: Before a pivotal battle, Constantine saw a cross in the sky and heard the words in this sign, conquer.

32
00:04:46,040 --> 00:04:51,700
Speaker 1: Following his victory, he became the first Roman Emperor to embrace Christianity.

33
00:04:52,740 --> 00:05:02,020
Speaker 1: In 313-ad- he issued the Edict of Milan, which legalized Christianity, ending decades of persecution.

34
00:05:03,060 --> 00:05:09,540
Speaker 1: Christianity, once a persecuted faith, now became the state supported religion.

35
00:05:09,860 --> 00:05:22,630
Speaker 1: And by 330 AD Constantine founded Constantinople, a new capital for the eastern Roman Empire, which would become a center for Christian governance and scholarship.

36
00:05:23,270 --> 00:05:31,990
Speaker 1: Constantine's conversion marked a turning point, bringing Christianity from the shadows into the light of the Roman Empire.

37
00:05:33,110 --> 00:05:45,120
Speaker 1: To understand why the Council of Nicaea was necessary, we must first understand what it was up against the heresies that challenged the essence of Christianity itself.

38
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:56,719
Speaker 1: First, docetism, the belief that Jesus only seemed human, that his body, his suffering, his death were all illusions.

39
00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:02,320
Speaker 1: Proponents said he was pure spirit, untouched by physical power.

